import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,a0 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BTg7EidG.js";const o="/athanor-docs/screens/city-jobs.png",n="/athanor-docs/screens/city-training.png",i="/athanor-docs/screens/city-build.png",l="/athanor-docs/screens/city-research.png",y=JSON.parse('{"title":"Cities & Holdings","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/cities.md","filePath":"guide/cities.md"}'),h={name:"guide/cities.md"};function d(g,e,c,p,u,m){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="cities-holdings" tabindex="-1">Cities &amp; Holdings <a class="header-anchor" href="#cities-holdings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cities &amp; Holdings&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Tap one of your cities and the city board opens straight away — with or without a party selected on it. Cities level from 1 to 3 — each level adds job slots, a floor to the skyline, and eventually unlocks the best projects.</p><h2 id="jobs" tabindex="-1">Jobs <a class="header-anchor" href="#jobs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Jobs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+o+'" alt="City jobs"></p><p>Spare <strong>Followers</strong> work the city&#39;s crafts: the <strong>Market</strong> (gold), <strong>Tavern</strong> (elixir), <strong>Temple</strong> (reagents), the <strong>Farms</strong> (food), the <strong>Magistrate</strong> (infamy) and — once you have raised one — the <strong>Bestiary</strong> (beast lore).</p><p>Every craft pays the same daily <strong>worth</strong>; only the coin differs. One keeper earns <strong>25 gold</strong>, or <strong>16 elixir</strong>, or <strong>12 reagents</strong>, or <strong>25 food</strong> — the same value at the rates guilds trade them. So posting a keeper is a choice of <em>what</em> your city makes, never of how much. The Magistrate is the exception: infamy is not traded, and it earns at its own slow pace. Infamy still matters — your deeds plus your magistrates decide how many new Followers arrive every seventh day.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">A posting is permanent</p><p>A Follower settles into their craft and does not come back out. Spend them like skill points: the number posted to a craft <strong>is that craft&#39;s level</strong>, and some things — beast lore especially — will not open until a craft has been raised high enough.</p></div><h2 id="walls-and-garrisons" tabindex="-1">Walls and garrisons <a class="header-anchor" href="#walls-and-garrisons" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Walls and garrisons&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Any holding takes at most <strong>10 soldiers</strong> — enough to hold a gate, not enough to make a fortress unassailable.</p><p>Defenders fight a little steadier for standing behind stone: a city or Grand Athanor is worth something on its own, and <strong>Walls</strong> raised in the city ring are worth more again, growing with their level. They also sharpen the drill of everyone posted behind them — in peacetime and during a defence.</p><h2 id="food-farms" tabindex="-1">Food &amp; farms <a class="header-anchor" href="#food-farms" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Food &amp; farms&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The faithful march on bread, not coin: every <strong>recruit costs 1 Follower, 40 food and 20 elixir</strong> — gold buys mercenaries, structures and training, never the faithful. Cities grow a base crop (villages a smaller one), Farm workers grow more, and for real granaries you settle <strong>farmsteads</strong>: hire a <strong>Farm Hand</strong> from the <strong>Recruit</strong> sheet at any of your cities (100🪙), march them out with a party, and on held, empty ground <strong>Settle a farmstead</strong> appears in the party&#39;s <strong>Skills</strong> sheet.</p><p>A hand carries <strong>seed for two fields</strong>. The first sowing leaves them with one charge, and the second uses them up — they stay behind to work that last field. If you have no hand to spare, a <strong>Charter of the Furrow</strong> in the satchel does the same job once, and any hero can present it.</p><p><strong>A farm hand never fights.</strong> They are on the party&#39;s roll, they travel with the column and they eat, but they are not marched into a battle line — and a holding garrisoned only by hands counts as <strong>undefended</strong>, so do not mistake one for a guard.</p><p>Each farmstead yields 10 food a day at full control, and like any holding of yours its fences <strong>ward the blight</strong>. A ring of farms is both a food supply and a wall.</p><h2 id="training" tabindex="-1">Training <a class="header-anchor" href="#training" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Training&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+n+'" alt="The training hall"></p><p>Every unit standing at the building — in a party or the garrison — can buy its next level for gold and elixir. The drillmaster only charges for what is left to learn: a soldier already three-quarters of the way to the next level pays a quarter of the list price, so battle scars are money in the bank. Training takes the day; the queue shows who&#39;s drilling. Keep a high-level veteran garrisoned and new recruits start at half their level.</p><p>Barracks train warriors, Laboratories train alchemists, Shadow Guilds train rogues; cities and Grand Athanors drill warriors. Tap any owned trainer building on the map to open its hall directly.</p><h2 id="build" tabindex="-1">Build <a class="header-anchor" href="#build" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Build&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+i+'" alt="The build queue"></p><p>Cities can raise new structures on free land beside them — a Barracks, Laboratory or Distillery, and at city level 3 a Shadow Guild. One project at a time; a few days each — and <strong>one of each per city</strong>. Once a structure stands, its row becomes the <strong>upgrade</strong> path instead: a structure can rise <strong>up to its city&#39;s level</strong> (raise the city first to raise its halls higher). Each level multiplies the structure&#39;s daily yield, and each level above the first cuts training costs at that hall by 15%. Tap the structure on the map to see exactly what an upgrade buys and costs.</p><h2 id="research" tabindex="-1">Research <a class="header-anchor" href="#research" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Research&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+l+'" alt="Skill research"></p><p>The Town Hall researches hero skills, one project at a time, guild-wide once learned. Nothing is granted: even a Lord&#39;s own class art (<em>Rally</em>, <em>Ambush</em>, <em>Transmute Blast</em>) is a study — the cheapest on the bench, and the one to start with. A <strong>level‑2 city</strong> unlocks the deeper studies (<em>Shield Wall</em>, <em>Plunder</em>, <em>Field Mending</em>) and a <strong>level‑3 city</strong> the masterworks (<em>Warcry</em>, <em>Overwhelm</em>, <em>Quicksilver March</em>). Once researched, assign the skill on any matching hero&#39;s sheet.</p><p>The Town Hall is not the only school. A <strong>Barracks</strong>, <strong>Laboratory</strong>, <strong>Shadow Guild</strong> or <strong>Bestiary</strong> teaches arts of its own trade that the hall never offers — which is a reason to raise them past drilling in them. The full tree lives in <a href="/athanor-docs/guide/skills.html">Skills &amp; Research</a>.</p><h2 id="defense" tabindex="-1">Defense <a class="header-anchor" href="#defense" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Defense&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If a visible enemy prowls within sight of an undefended holding, the game warns you and offers an <strong>emergency muster</strong> — recruits or mercenaries straight onto the walls. The locate button&#39;s jump list shows every city, flagging any whose walls stand empty.</p><p>Garrison duty pays on its own, too: a soldier standing a wall earns a slow trickle of <strong>experience every day</strong> — the wall is a drill yard. Heroes learn from deeds, not drills, but a green recruit posted to a quiet hold comes back sharper. Between the drills and their wages, a standing garrison keeps your surplus working instead of piling up.</p>',29)])])}const w=t(h,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,w as default};
